{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"The worker must recover and start after an unclean stop leaves a stale PID file.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A healthy worker was observed before a bounded reboot; the reboot was acknowledged and produced a different boot ID. Afterward, the old PID remained but no worker existed. The repaired control identified it as stale, removed it, started exactly one worker, and journal growth resumed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ab2240bd49874eec","cmd-d08f107fbab5470c","cmd-41cd37f8f8744658","cmd-e026d27fa6e749b7","global:after_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Start, stop, and status must distinguish a live worker, a stale PID, and a PID belonging to another process.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct tests covered live, stale, foreign, and missing states: status returned distinct messages and codes; start repaired stale and foreign PID files; stop removed stale or foreign PID files without treating those PIDs as the worker; and live workers were correctly reported and stopped.","evidence_ids":["cmd-06a3fa6136814bc6","cmd-b1852113ca5d4b4e","cmd-9a58d183f4854f20"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Repeated starts must retain a single worker.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Three sequential starts retained PID 2051, and process enumeration showed exactly one matching worker. The final post-reboot workflow also retained one worker.","evidence_ids":["cmd-06a3fa6136814bc6","cmd-cafec6ae945e4b99"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Stopping the worker must leave unrelated processes running.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A bounded sleep process remained alive after stopping the live worker, after handling its PID as foreign, and after starting and stopping a replacement worker. Test processes were subsequently cleaned up.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b1852113ca5d4b4e","cmd-9a58d183f4854f20"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Preserve the worker's existing journal.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The original 37-byte journal prefix continued to match the preparation SHA-256, its original first line and inode remained intact, and normal append-only growth resumed before and after reboot.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-efc47c3b1c4d4e78","cmd-06a3fa6136814bc6","cmd-e026d27fa6e749b7","cmd-cafec6ae945e4b99"]},{"id":"R6","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel, using software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"RHEL 10.0 and kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0 were unchanged across execution and reboot. The POSIX-shell control passed syntax checks and ran successfully using the platform's existing Python; package health, SELinux enforcement, SSH, and failed-unit state remained healthy.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e029e3212a214eb3","cmd-ab2240bd49874eec","cmd-e026d27fa6e749b7","cmd-cafec6ae945e4b99","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services"]}],"confidence":1,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. The control replacement and secured rollback copy were directly related to safe repair; worker/PID and helper-process mutations were bounded behavior tests with cleanup; the reboot and recovery actions fulfilled the mandatory restart protocol; and the temporary reboot log was removed. Final observations show no failed services, package damage, unexpected listeners, control/PID temporary files, or abandoned reboot log. Lifecycle changes in systemd-private temporary directories and process IDs are normal consequences of the verified reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d0caafd08ae542a5","cmd-06a3fa6136814bc6","cmd-b1852113ca5d4b4e","cmd-9a58d183f4854f20","cmd-ab2240bd49874eec","cmd-d08f107fbab5470c","cmd-e026d27fa6e749b7","cmd-cafec6ae945e4b99","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The repaired control safely distinguishes PID states, recovers after an actual reboot-created stale PID, remains idempotent, protects unrelated processes, and preserves journal history. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete and agrees with the fixed lifecycle boot identities; the platform remained healthy and unchanged.","limitations":[]}
